    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d148">

                <pb facs="0258" n="156" sameAs="frus:frus1935v01/pg156"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">500.A15A5/571: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the
                        Ambassador in the United Kingdom</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Bingham</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1935-12-06T17:00:00-05:00">December 6, 1935—5
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>381. Embassy at Tokyo in telegram No. 231, December 6, 9 a.m.,<anchor n="96"
                        xml:id="d148-f1"/> reports “newspapers yesterday and today have carried
                    stories to the effect that the Japanese delegates to the London Conference would
                    make proposals in regard to the Four Power Treaty<anchor n="97" xml:id="d148-f2"
                    /> and in regard to fortifications in the Pacific. I took occasion to inquire at
                    the Foreign Office and was informed that there is no basis for these reports and
                    that the Japanese delegates are not authorized to introduce political subjects
                    at London.”</p>
                <p>Please inform Naval Delegation.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
